The whole library is two functions, and the translation hinges on two C quirks that I reproduced rather than fixed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **Sign-extension in [38;5;10mprintf("%02x\n", charHex)[0m.** [38;5;10mchar[0m is promoted to [38;5;10mint[0m before reaching the variadic call, and [38;5;10m%02x[0m then reads it as [38;5;10munsigned int[0m. On x86_64 Linux [38;5;10mchar[0m is signed, so [38;5;10m0x80[0m prints as [38;5;10mffffff80[0m, not [38;5;10m80[0m. The [38;5;10m02[0m width is only a [3mminimum[23m, so it never truncates. I kept this by promoting with [38;5;10mcharHex as c_int[0m (which follows [38;5;10mc_char[0m's target signedness) and calling libc's [38;5;10mprintf[0m directly, so both the formatting and stdout's block-buffering behavior match — Rust's [38;5;10mprint![0m uses a separate line-buffered handle and would interleave differently if a caller mixes C and Rust output.[0m[0m
- **Truncation in [38;5;10mchar result = data + 1[0m.** The addition happens in [38;5;10mint[0m and is then narrowed back to [38;5;10mchar[0m, so [38;5;10m0x7f[0m wraps to [38;5;10m-128[0m ([38;5;10mffffff80[0m) and [38;5;10m0xff[0m wraps to [38;5;10m0[0m ([38;5;10m00[0m). [38;5;10mwrapping_add(1)[0m gives exactly that.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Both [38;5;10mdriver[0m and [38;5;10mprintHexCharLine[0m are exported. [38;5;10mprintHexCharLine[0m isn't in [38;5;10mdriver.h[0m, but it's non-[38;5;10mstatic[0m in the C, so it's a visible symbol in the shared object; I matched that. There are no namespace-renaming macros in the header, so the linker symbols are the plain source names — confirmed with [38;5;10mnm -D[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One note on a judgment call: I set [38;5;10mpanic = "abort"[0m in the release profile. Unwinding out of an [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m function is undefined behavior, and while nothing here can panic, aborting is the safer default for a cdylib. Say the word if you'd rather keep the default unwind behavior.[0m[0m
